  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:10 AM EDT...Current GOES 16 WV imagery shows \nseveral upper impulses tracking over the Great Lakes around the \nperiphery of a closed upper low located near James Bay. This upper \nlow is vertically stacked with an occluded sfc low, with the \ntrailing cold front extending through the eastern Great Lakes. Ahead \nof this front, relatively warm lake surface temps, cooling aloft, \nand the left exit of an upper jet streak have allowed for showers to \ndevelop and track eastwards into our region. These showers are more \nnumerous than previously expected, so have increased PoPs for the \nnorthern half of our area through the next few hours. There was\nmore embedded convection off to our west, and we have seen a \nfew lightning flashes over the southern ADKs earlier tonight, \nbut given lack of instability across our region the chances for \nadditional thunder look low. This initial batch showers should \nmove off to our east towards sunrise, but some additional \nisolated showers may be possible through today due to westerly \nflow over the Great Lakes, cool temperatures aloft, and a couple\nof additional upper shortwaves tracking through the mean flow. \nBest chance will be for the southern ADKs and southern Greens \nwhere westerly upslope will locally enhance lift. It will also \nbecome quite breezy behind the cold front this morning. Tight \npressure gradient and deep mixing within the low-level cold \nadvection regime will allow for gusts up to 20-30 kt, strongest \nwhere flow is channeled down the Mohawk Valley, through the \nCapital District, and into the Berkshires. We accordingly bumped\nup winds quite a bit from the NBM in coordination with \nneighboring WFOs. \n\nShower chances generally diminish after sunset this evening, \nalthough some additional lake/upslope enhanced showers may redevelop \ntomorrow over the ADKs/southern Greens as we will still be close to \nthe upper trough and associated cold pool aloft. Any showers should \nbe light, with less coverage compared to this afternoon. Tomorrow \nwill still see breezy W/NW winds, albeit not as strong as this \nafternoon. With sfc high pressure building overhead Friday night, \nfavorable radiational cooling conditions will likely allow temps to \ndrop into the 30s to low 40s for most areas. Some patchy frost will \nbe possible in the high terrain areas, although the growing season \nhas already ended there. The sfc high continues to strengthen \nthrough the first half of the weekend beneath an amplifying \nupper ridge, so we should see large-scale subsidence leading to \ncontinued tranquil weather. Saturday looks like a beautiful fall\nday with light winds and highs in the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By the second half of the weekend, the remnants of \nwhat is currently Hurricane Isaias in the Gulf will track towards \nour region. There remains some uncertainty in the track of this \nsystem as it approaches our area, especially with regards to the\nnorthern extent of the precip shield given that it will be \nrunning into upper ridging and the strong sfc high to our east. \nThere are plenty of deterministic and ensemble members that have\nour entire area receiving rain, although the probability for\nappreciable rain is highest south of I-90. Given very dry \nantecedent conditions (southern areas will be going on close to \n2 weeks without appreciable rain), any rain that falls will be \nvery beneficial. NBM probabilities for >1\" continue to range \nfrom <10% across our northern zones to around 30% near I-84, \nwith probabilities for >2\" under 5% area-wide. Therefore, not \nexpecting any flooding issues with this rain despite its \ntropical origins. Showers should come to an end from W/NW to \nE/SE through the day Monday. A strong cold front then looks to \ntrack through the region sometime TUE/WED next week, bringing \nadditional chances for showers and another shot of cool air to \nthe region.",
